---
title: "Amortized R(t): behavioral SIR models across all US states"
description: >
  Fit three competing epidemic models to daily reported cases in every US
  state, summarize the 120-day case curve with a learned embedding network,
  and ask which behavioral mechanism explains the shape of the first
  SARS-CoV-2 wave.
output: rmarkdown::html_vignette
vignette: >
  %\VignetteIndexEntry{Amortized R(t): behavioral SIR models across all US states}
  %\VignetteEngine{knitr::rmarkdown_notangle}
  %\VignetteEncoding{UTF-8}
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
# This vignette is precomputed: see vignettes/precompute.R. Edit the .Rmd.orig,
# re-run the precompute script, and commit the regenerated .Rmd and figures.
# Regenerating it needs internet access (to pull case counts and population
# data) as well as torch.
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE, comment = "#>", error = FALSE,
  fig.path = "figures/sir-rt-", fig.width = 6.5, fig.height = 4.5,
  dpi = 96, fig.retina = NULL
)
set.seed(1)
```

`vignette("sir-epidemic")` fits a stochastic SIR outbreak with a *constant*
contact rate \(\beta\). Real epidemics do not hold still, and the interesting
question is *why* they move. Control measures, voluntary behavior change, and
depleting susceptibles all push transmission around over the course of an
outbreak, and different mechanisms leave different fingerprints on the case
curve.

This vignette fits **three competing models** of that mechanism to daily
reported cases in all 50 US states plus Washington, DC, over the first 120
days of the SARS-CoV-2 pandemic (2020-01-21 to 2020-05-19). Two of them are
behavioral-feedback models taken from Gozzi, Perra and Vespignani's
comparative study (2025, *PNAS* 122(24) e2421993122), where transmission
responds to what people can see happening around them. The third is a
phenomenological baseline that simply lets \(\beta\) take a different constant
value in each of a few time regimes. Each is trained **once**, amortized over
the prior, and then conditioned on 51 different state curves for the price of
a forward pass each. That is what makes a three-model, 51-jurisdiction
comparison affordable at all.

Along the way the vignette answers the question public-health teams actually
care about — how did the **effective reproduction number**
\(R_e(t) = \beta(t) S(t) / (N \gamma)\) evolve? — and shows two pieces of
`neuralsbi` machinery that matter for time-series inference: an
`embedding_mlp()` summary network, and a prior predictive check that catches
the kind of model error no amount of training will fix.

## The data: daily reported cases in the first 120 days

We use the New York Times' state-level COVID-19 case counts, which start on
2020-01-21 — the date of the first confirmed US case (Snohomish County,
Washington) — and US Census population estimates.

```{r}
cases_url <- "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nytimes/covid-19-data/master/us-states.csv"
pop_url <- "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/COVID19Tracking/associated-data/master/us_census_data/us_census_2018_population_estimates_states.csv"

raw_cases <- read.csv(cases_url)
pop_table <- read.csv(pop_url)

raw_cases$date <- as.Date(raw_cases$date)
start_date <- as.Date("2020-01-21")
days <- 120L
end_date <- start_date + days - 1L
window <- raw_cases[raw_cases$date >= start_date & raw_cases$date <= end_date, ]

# the 50 states + DC only -- drop territories (Puerto Rico is the only one the
# population table also carries, so it needs an explicit exclusion)
state_names <- sort(setdiff(intersect(unique(window$state), pop_table$state_name),
                            "Puerto Rico"))
window <- window[window$state %in% state_names, ]
length(state_names)
```

The NYT series is cumulative, and it is not monotone: data revisions
occasionally push the running total down. We difference it to daily new
cases, clipping the revision artifacts at zero.

```{r}
daily_cases <- function(state_df) {
  full <- data.frame(date = seq(start_date, end_date, by = "day"))
  full <- merge(full, state_df[, c("date", "cases")], by = "date", all.x = TRUE)
  full$cases[is.na(full$cases)] <- 0
  full$cases <- cummax(full$cases)             # enforce a monotone cumulative count
  new <- c(full$cases[1], diff(full$cases))
  new[new < 0] <- 0                            # data-revision artifacts
  new
}

case_daily <- t(sapply(state_names, function(s) daily_cases(window[window$state == s, ])))
rownames(case_daily) <- state_names
pop <- stats::setNames(pop_table$population, pop_table$state_name)[state_names]
dim(case_daily)
range(pop)
```

Daily counts carry a strong weekday cycle — fewer tests processed and fewer
results reported at weekends — that no SIR model generates and none of these
models should be asked to fit. We take a **centred 7-day mean** as the summary
statistic, and the key point is that we will apply the *same* smoother to
simulated data. The network then learns \(p(\theta \mid s(x))\) for a summary
\(s\) that means the same thing on both sides; smoothing only the observation
would be a fudge, and the simulated and observed data would no longer be
comparable.

```{r}
smooth7 <- function(m) {
  m <- if (is.matrix(m)) m else matrix(m, nrow = 1)
  out <- m
  for (i in seq_len(ncol(m))) {
    idx <- max(1L, i - 3L):min(ncol(m), i + 3L)
    out[, i] <- rowMeans(m[, idx, drop = FALSE])
  }
  out
}
case_daily_s <- smooth7(case_daily)
```

State populations span two orders of magnitude, from Wyoming
(`r format(pop["Wyoming"], big.mark = ",")`) up to California
(`r format(max(pop), big.mark = ",")`). That range matters below: population
size is a *known* covariate, not something we infer, but one amortized
posterior has to condition on it correctly for a 39-million-person state and
a 600,000-person state alike.

```{r national-curve, fig.alt = "National new reported cases per day, 7-day rolling average, first 120 days."}
national <- aggregate(cases ~ date, data = window, sum)
national <- national[order(national$date), ]
national$new <- pmax(c(national$cases[1], diff(national$cases)), 0)
national$new7 <- as.numeric(smooth7(national$new))
national$day <- as.numeric(national$date - start_date) + 1

plot(national$date, national$new7, type = "l", lwd = 2,
     xlab = "date", ylab = "new cases/day (7-day average)",
     main = "US reported cases, first 120 days")
```

Notice the shape: a steep rise through March, then a **broad, flat top**
running from early April to the end of the window. That plateau is the thing
the three models below have to explain, and it is a real discriminator. A
plain SIR epidemic with a fixed contact rate produces a sharp peak, not a
shoulder.

## Three mechanisms, one core

All three models share the same discrete-time, binomial-transition SIR core
used in `vignette("sir-epidemic")`: susceptible, infected, and recovered
counts update via `rbinom()`, so the process is genuinely stochastic and
there is no tractable likelihood for the reported-case curve. Susceptibles
deplete — every infection removes someone from \(S\), and that feedback is
what separates the *effective* reproduction number from the contact rate.
Three ingredients are shared by all three models and are worth stating before
the mechanisms that differ.

**The epidemic starts when it starts.** The day the virus arrives, \(t_0\),
and the number of infections \(I_0\) that arrive with it are **inferred**, not
fixed. Seeding every state with a couple of infections on 2020-01-21 gets the
epidemiology wrong twice over. It is wrong about the world — Washington's
outbreak was seeded in January, West Virginia did not report a case until
March 17 — and it is wrong about what a two-infection seed does to a 120-day
simulation. A supercritical branching process started from two individuals
has a substantial extinction probability and, conditional on survival, a
final size spread over orders of magnitude; the *timing* of takeoff then
wanders by weeks. Replicate simulations at a single fixed \(\theta\) differ by
a factor of several thousand at the peak. Inferring \((t_0, I_0)\) pins the
phase with a parameter instead of leaving it to luck, and it is the more
faithful model besides: states were seeded by many independent importations,
not by a single index case.

**Ascertainment is pinned by seroprevalence, not left free.** Only a fraction
\(\rho\) of infections became a confirmed case in spring 2020. \(\rho\) is
inferred, but with an *informative* prior, and that is not optional. Reported
case counts identify the product \(\rho \times \text{incidence}\), not either
factor: a small, well-ascertained epidemic and an enormous, barely-ascertained
one produce the same curve. Left to itself the fit takes the second branch,
because susceptible depletion is then available to flatten the peak cheaply,
which drives \(\rho\) into the low thousandths and the attack rate close to 1.
Serology settles it from outside the case data: CDC's large-scale
commercial-lab study put spring-2020 infections at roughly 6 to 24 times
reported cases across 10 US sites (Havers et al. 2020, *JAMA Internal
Medicine*), i.e. \(\rho\) between about 0.04 and 0.17. We encode that as a
logit-normal prior centered at 0.10 with 95% mass in \([0.05, 0.19]\).

**Population size \(N\) is a known covariate.** The simulator draws it from a
distribution spanning real US state sizes during training, and the estimator
conditions on \(\log N\) alongside the case curve, so one trained network
generalizes from Wyoming to California. The recovery rate \(\gamma\) is
**fixed**: with only case counts the generation interval is not separately
identifiable from \(\beta(t)\), so we set \(1/\gamma = 7\) days, in line with
early estimates of the COVID-19 serial interval (Li et al. 2020, *NEJM*).

```{r}
library(neuralsbi)

gamma_fixed <- 1 / 7
t_grid <- seq_len(days)
PER <- 1e5                      # behavioral drivers are per 100,000 people
```

### Model 1 — piecewise-constant \(\beta(t)\) (the phenomenological baseline)

The first model makes no claim about *why* transmission changed: it lets
\(\log\beta\) take a different constant value in each of a few time regimes,
blending between them over a smooth, roughly one-week logistic transition
(policy roll-out and behavior change unfold over days, not instantaneously).
It is a flexible curve fit, and it is the arm the other two have to beat.

How many regimes, and where do the boundaries go? We answer that from the
national curve rather than by assumption, fitting a continuous piecewise-linear
regression to `log(new7)` and selecting breakpoints by BIC.

```{r knot-selection}
nat <- national[national$new7 > 0, ]
y <- log(nat$new7); day <- nat$day; n_obs <- length(y)

fit_bic <- function(taus) {
  X <- cbind(1, day, sapply(taus, function(tau) pmax(day - tau, 0)))
  fit <- lm.fit(X, y)
  if (any(is.na(fit$coefficients))) return(NULL)
  rss <- sum(fit$residuals^2); p <- ncol(X)
  list(bic = n_obs * log(rss / n_obs) + p * log(n_obs), taus = taus)
}

cand <- seq(10, 110, by = 5)
best_by_k <- lapply(0:6, function(k) {
  if (k == 0) return(fit_bic(numeric(0)))
  combs <- combn(cand, k); best <- NULL
  for (j in seq_len(ncol(combs))) {
    r <- fit_bic(combs[, j])
    if (!is.null(r) && (is.null(best) || r$bic < best$bic)) best <- r
  }
  best
})

knot_table <- data.frame(
  breakpoints = 0:6,
  bic = sapply(best_by_k, `[[`, "bic"),
  taus = sapply(best_by_k, function(b) paste(b$taus, collapse = ", ")))
knot_table$delta_bic <- c(NA, -diff(knot_table$bic))
knot_table
```

BIC keeps improving out to 6 breakpoints, so raw BIC alone does not pick a
winner — a second signal does. Compare the breakpoint sets themselves: going
from 2 to 3 breakpoints *refines* the previous solution ({30, 70} becomes
{25, 40, 70}), but going from 3 to 4 does not — {25, 40, 70} is replaced
wholesale by {15, 20, 40, 70}, dropping day 25 and inserting two breakpoints
only 5 days apart, both inside the window where national counts were in the
single or low double digits. That is the signature of a search fitting noise
in small counts rather than finding new structure: real inflections give
stable refinements as `k` grows, spurious ones make the whole configuration
jump. We take **3 breakpoints** — 4 constant segments — as the last stable,
well-supported fit.

```{r}
knot_days <- c(1, sort(best_by_k[[4]]$taus), days)
knot_days
n_segments <- length(knot_days) - 1L
nominal_dur <- diff(knot_days)
total_days <- sum(nominal_dur)
transition_scale <- 7 / 4
```

The breakpoints get **wiggle room per state**: community transmission did not
take hold, and stay-at-home orders did not land, on the same calendar day
everywhere. Rather than move each breakpoint independently — which risks two
adjacent breakpoints crossing — we put a prior on each regime's *duration* and
take the breakpoints as cumulative sums, so the regimes tile the window in
order for every draw. Only 3 of the 4 durations are free: the simulator
rescales them to span the window exactly, so their common scale does nothing.
Parameterizing the free directions directly (three log-ratios against the last
regime) keeps every coordinate of \(\theta\) identified — a fourth would be
pure noise for the flow to model, and SBC would not catch it, because an
unidentified coordinate returns its prior and looks perfectly calibrated.

```{r}
beta_piecewise <- function(log_beta_segments, log_dur_ratio) {
  n <- nrow(log_beta_segments)
  w <- cbind(exp(log_dur_ratio), 1)
  dur <- w / rowSums(w) * total_days
  tau <- knot_days[1] + t(apply(dur, 1, cumsum))[, seq_len(n_segments - 1L), drop = FALSE]
  w_ext <- c(list(matrix(1, n, days)),
             lapply(seq_len(n_segments - 1L), function(k) {
               outer(tau[, k], t_grid, function(a, t) plogis((t - a) / transition_scale))
             }),
             list(matrix(0, n, days)))
  exp(Reduce(`+`, lapply(seq_len(n_segments), function(j) {
    (w_ext[[j]] - w_ext[[j + 1]]) * log_beta_segments[, j]
  })))
}
```

### Models 2 and 3 — behavioral feedback

Gozzi, Perra and Vespignani (2025) compare mechanistic behavioral epidemic
models against data-driven ones across nine cities and the first COVID-19
wave. Their two *analytical* feedback models need no external mobility data —
transmission responds to the epidemic's own reported output — which makes both
of them self-contained simulators, exactly what SBI wants.

**EFB (effective force of infection).** The force of infection is divided by a
factor that grows with what people have recently seen and with what they have
seen cumulatively:
\[
\lambda(t) = \beta_0 \frac{I(t)}{N} \cdot
  \frac{1}{1 + \xi\, c(t-1) + \psi \sum_{t' < t} c(t')} .
\]
\(\xi\) is reactivity to the current news, \(\psi\) to accumulated experience.
Six parameters in total.

**CBF (compartmental behavioral feedback).** Susceptibles move into a
risk-averse compartment \(S_B\) at rate \(\beta_B (1 - e^{-g\,c(t-1)})\),
drift back at rate \(\mu_B\), and while in \(S_B\) their force of infection is
scaled by \(r < 1\). Behavior here is a *state* of the population that takes
time to build and to decay, rather than an instantaneous function of the
news. Eight parameters.

Two adaptations to the paper's specification, both forced by what we are
doing here. The paper drives behavior off reported **deaths**; we drive it off
reported **cases**, which is the only outcome this model generates and was
itself a headline number in spring 2020. And we express the driver \(c(t)\)
**per 100,000 people** rather than as a raw count, because a single amortized
posterior has to serve Wyoming and California: behavior responds to perceived
local risk, which scales with per-capita incidence. With a raw-count driver
the same \(\xi\) would mean drastically different behavior in a small state
and a large one, and no single \(\xi\) could fit both.

```{r}
draw_N <- function(n, N_fixed) {
  if (is.null(N_fixed)) exp(stats::runif(n, log(5e5), log(4e7))) else rep(N_fixed, n)
}

# x = log(N) followed by the 7-day-smoothed daily reported cases, log1p'd.
pack <- function(N, reported, S_frac, beta_eff, full) {
  x <- cbind(log(N), log1p(smooth7(reported)))
  if (!full) return(x)
  list(x = x, reported = reported, S_frac = S_frac, beta_eff = beta_eff)
}

# shared: introduce the epidemic at t0 with a Poisson(seed_mean) seed
seed_step <- function(t, t0, seed_mean, S, I, seeded) {
  fresh <- !seeded & (t >= t0)
  if (any(fresh)) {
    s0 <- pmin(stats::rpois(sum(fresh), seed_mean[fresh]), S[fresh])
    I[fresh] <- I[fresh] + s0
    S[fresh] <- S[fresh] - s0
    seeded[fresh] <- TRUE
  }
  list(S = S, I = I, seeded = seeded)
}
```

```{r}
sim_pw <- function(theta, N_fixed = NULL, full = FALSE) {
  theta <- matrix(as.numeric(theta), ncol = 10L)
  n <- nrow(theta)
  beta_t <- beta_piecewise(theta[, 1:4, drop = FALSE], theta[, 5:7, drop = FALSE])
  t0 <- 1 + (days - 1) * plogis(theta[, 8])
  seed_mean <- exp(theta[, 9]); rho <- plogis(theta[, 10])
  N <- draw_N(n, N_fixed)

  S <- N; I <- rep(0, n); seeded <- rep(FALSE, n)
  reported <- matrix(0, n, days); S_frac <- matrix(0, n, days)
  n_sub <- 2L; dt <- 1 / n_sub; p_rec <- 1 - exp(-gamma_fixed * dt)
  for (t in t_grid) {
    st <- seed_step(t, t0, seed_mean, S, I, seeded); S <- st$S; I <- st$I; seeded <- st$seeded
    H <- rep(0, n)
    for (s in seq_len(n_sub)) {
      p_inf <- pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-beta_t[, t] * I / N * dt), 0), 1)
      dSI <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(S), 0), p_inf)
      dIR <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(I), 0), p_rec)
      S <- S - dSI; I <- I + dSI - dIR; H <- H + dSI    # susceptibles deplete
    }
    S_frac[, t] <- S / N
    reported[, t] <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(H), 0), rho)
  }
  pack(N, reported, S_frac, beta_t, full)
}

sim_efb <- function(theta, N_fixed = NULL, full = FALSE) {
  theta <- matrix(as.numeric(theta), ncol = 6L)
  n <- nrow(theta)
  beta0 <- exp(theta[, 1]); xi <- exp(theta[, 2]); psi <- exp(theta[, 3])
  t0 <- 1 + (days - 1) * plogis(theta[, 4])
  seed_mean <- exp(theta[, 5]); rho <- plogis(theta[, 6])
  N <- draw_N(n, N_fixed)

  S <- N; I <- rep(0, n); seeded <- rep(FALSE, n)
  c_prev <- rep(0, n); C_cum <- rep(0, n)
  reported <- matrix(0, n, days); S_frac <- matrix(0, n, days)
  beta_eff <- matrix(0, n, days)
  n_sub <- 2L; dt <- 1 / n_sub; p_rec <- 1 - exp(-gamma_fixed * dt)
  for (t in t_grid) {
    st <- seed_step(t, t0, seed_mean, S, I, seeded); S <- st$S; I <- st$I; seeded <- st$seeded
    bt <- beta0 / (1 + xi * c_prev + psi * C_cum)      # the behavioral response
    beta_eff[, t] <- bt
    H <- rep(0, n)
    for (s in seq_len(n_sub)) {
      p_inf <- pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-bt * I / N * dt), 0), 1)
      dSI <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(S), 0), p_inf)
      dIR <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(I), 0), p_rec)
      S <- S - dSI; I <- I + dSI - dIR; H <- H + dSI
    }
    S_frac[, t] <- S / N
    rep_t <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(H), 0), rho)
    reported[, t] <- rep_t
    c_prev <- rep_t / N * PER                          # per 100k, so one xi fits every state
    C_cum <- C_cum + c_prev
  }
  pack(N, reported, S_frac, beta_eff, full)
}

sim_cbf <- function(theta, N_fixed = NULL, full = FALSE) {
  theta <- matrix(as.numeric(theta), ncol = 8L)
  n <- nrow(theta)
  beta0 <- exp(theta[, 1]); betaB <- exp(theta[, 2]); g <- exp(theta[, 3])
  r <- plogis(theta[, 4]); muB <- exp(theta[, 5])
  t0 <- 1 + (days - 1) * plogis(theta[, 6])
  seed_mean <- exp(theta[, 7]); rho <- plogis(theta[, 8])
  N <- draw_N(n, N_fixed)

  S <- N; SB <- rep(0, n); I <- rep(0, n); seeded <- rep(FALSE, n)
  c_prev <- rep(0, n)
  reported <- matrix(0, n, days); S_frac <- matrix(0, n, days)
  beta_eff <- matrix(0, n, days)
  n_sub <- 2L; dt <- 1 / n_sub; p_rec <- 1 - exp(-gamma_fixed * dt)
  for (t in t_grid) {
    st <- seed_step(t, t0, seed_mean, S, I, seeded); S <- st$S; I <- st$I; seeded <- st$seeded
    pB <- betaB * (1 - exp(-g * c_prev))               # awareness-driven uptake
    H <- rep(0, n)
    for (s in seq_len(n_sub)) {
      lam <- beta0 * I / N
      # a susceptible faces competing risks -- infection or adopting safer
      # behavior -- so draw the total departures and split them, rather than
      # letting whichever event is applied first pre-empt the other
      tot_haz <- lam + pB
      dS <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(S), 0),
                          pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-tot_haz * dt), 0), 1))
      dSI <- stats::rbinom(n, dS, ifelse(tot_haz > 0, lam / tot_haz, 0))
      dSB <- dS - dSI
      dBI <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(SB), 0),
                           pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-r * lam * dt), 0), 1))
      dBS <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(SB) - dBI, 0),
                           pmin(pmax(1 - exp(-muB * dt), 0), 1))
      dIR <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(I), 0), p_rec)
      S <- S - dS + dBS; SB <- SB + dSB - dBI - dBS
      I <- I + dSI + dBI - dIR; H <- H + dSI + dBI
    }
    S_frac[, t] <- (S + SB) / N
    # transmission rate averaged over the susceptible pool, for reporting R_e
    beta_eff[, t] <- beta0 * (S + r * SB) / pmax(S + SB, 1)
    rep_t <- stats::rbinom(n, pmax(round(H), 0), rho)
    reported[, t] <- rep_t
    c_prev <- rep_t / N * PER
  }
  pack(N, reported, S_frac, beta_eff, full)
}
```

The priors. Every parameter is unconstrained on \(\mathbb{R}\) — the log/logit
transforms mean a single Gaussian prior with no truncated support, so the
posterior needs no rejection sampling for leakage. Naming each `mean` vector
carries the names through every posterior draw, SBC result, and diagnostic
plot. Note that \(\beta_0\) means different things in the two families: in the
piecewise model it is an *effective* rate per regime, so its prior is centred
on \(R = 2\) and deliberately wide in both directions; in the behavioral
models it is the *intrinsic* transmission rate with the decline explained
mechanistically, so it is centred on \(R_0 = 3\), the wild-type value.

```{r}
prior_pw <- prior_normal(
  mean = stats::setNames(
    c(rep(log(0.28), n_segments),
      log(nominal_dur[-n_segments] / nominal_dur[n_segments]),
      qlogis(35 / days), log(30), qlogis(0.10)),
    c(paste0("beta[", seq_len(n_segments), "]"),
      paste0("d[", seq_len(n_segments - 1L), "]"), "t[0]", "I[0]", "rho")),
  sd = c(rep(0.6, n_segments), rep(0.35, n_segments - 1L), 1.1, 1.2, 0.4))

prior_efb <- prior_normal(
  mean = stats::setNames(
    c(log(3 * gamma_fixed), log(0.05), log(0.001),
      qlogis(35 / days), log(30), qlogis(0.10)),
    c("beta[0]", "xi", "psi", "t[0]", "I[0]", "rho")),
  sd = c(0.30, 1.5, 1.5, 1.1, 1.2, 0.4))

prior_cbf <- prior_normal(
  mean = stats::setNames(
    c(log(3 * gamma_fixed), log(0.1), log(0.5), qlogis(0.3), log(0.02),
      qlogis(35 / days), log(30), qlogis(0.10)),
    c("beta[0]", "beta[B]", "g", "r", "mu[B]", "t[0]", "I[0]", "rho")),
  sd = c(0.30, 1.0, 1.5, 1.0, 1.0, 1.1, 1.2, 0.4))
```

## Summarizing 120 days with an embedding network

The observation is `r days + 1` numbers: \(\log N\) plus a 120-day case curve.
Feeding that straight into the flow wastes capacity — the flow would have to
learn, from the conditioning path alone, that neighboring days are nearly
redundant. The alternative most tutorials reach for is to hand-pick summary
statistics (bin the curve into weeks, take a growth rate and a peak height),
which throws away whatever the hand-picked set failed to capture.

`embedding_mlp()` does the compression by learning it. It is a small MLP
mapping the standardized 121-dimensional observation to `output_dim` features,
trained **jointly** with the density estimator, so the features are optimized
for exactly one thing: predicting \(\theta\). The estimator then conditions on
those features rather than on the raw curve.

```{r}
embed <- embedding_mlp(output_dim = 12L, hidden = c(64L, 64L))
embed
```

## Check the prior predictive before training anything

Amortized NPE learns \(p(\theta \mid x)\) over the *prior predictive*
distribution of \(x\). Data that the prior predictive essentially never
produces sit in a region where the trained network is extrapolating, and its
posterior there is not to be trusted — no amount of training fixes that. So
before spending a simulation budget, check that each model can produce curves
like the ones we intend to condition on.

```{r prior-predictive}
set.seed(11)
n_pp <- 1500
sims <- list(PW = list(sim = sim_pw, prior = prior_pw),
             EFB = list(sim = sim_efb, prior = prior_efb),
             CBF = list(sim = sim_cbf, prior = prior_cbf))

pp_check <- function(m, state) {
  theta <- sample_prior(m$prior, n_pp)
  lx <- m$sim(theta, N_fixed = pop[[state]])[, -1, drop = FALSE]   # already log1p
  d <- rowMeans(abs(lx - rep(log1p(case_daily_s[state, ]), each = n_pp)))
  mean(d < 1)
}
round(sapply(sims, function(m) sapply(c("New York", "Michigan", "West Virginia"),
                                      function(s) pp_check(m, s))), 4)
```

The number to watch is what fraction of prior draws land within a factor of
\(e\) of the observed curve, averaged over the 120 days. It is small — these
priors are deliberately vague — but it is not a rounding error, so the
observations sit inside the bulk of each prior predictive rather than out in
its tail. Fixing the seeding day at day 1 instead of inferring it fails
exactly this check: West Virginia's seven empty weeks can then only be
produced by a sub-threshold contact rate, which usually means extinction, so
the observed curve drops out of the band's lower edge.

```{r prior-pred-plot, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 4, fig.alt = "Prior predictive envelope for the CBF model on a log scale with two observed state curves inside it."}
theta_pp <- sample_prior(prior_cbf, n_pp)
mi <- expm1(sim_cbf(theta_pp, N_fixed = pop[["Michigan"]])[, -1, drop = FALSE])
env <- apply(log1p(mi), 2, stats::quantile, c(0.05, 0.5, 0.95))
dates <- start_date + t_grid - 1

plot(dates, env[2, ], type = "n", ylim = range(env), xlab = "date (2020)",
     ylab = "log(1 + cases/day)", main = "CBF prior predictive, Michigan-sized population")
polygon(c(dates, rev(dates)), c(env[1, ], rev(env[3, ])), col = "grey85", border = NA)
lines(dates, env[2, ], lwd = 2, col = "grey40")
lines(dates, log1p(case_daily_s["Michigan", ]), lwd = 2, col = "firebrick")
lines(dates, log1p(case_daily_s["West Virginia", ]), lwd = 2, col = "steelblue")
legend("topleft", bty = "n", lwd = 2, col = c("grey40", "firebrick", "steelblue"),
       legend = c("prior predictive median (90% band)", "Michigan", "West Virginia"))
```

## Train all three

One fit per model, each amortized across the whole prior — including the full
range of state population sizes — and reused for all 51 jurisdictions. Train
once, condition 51 times for the price of a forward pass each: that is what
makes running three models across every state affordable.

```{r fit-npe}
n_sim <- 15000
fits <- lapply(sims, function(m) {
  npe(m$prior, m$sim, n_simulations = n_sim, density_estimator = "maf",
      embedding_net = embed, max_epochs = 300, n_restarts = 1,
      seed = 1, verbose = FALSE)
})
fits$CBF
```

## Validate before trusting a single curve

Simulation-based calibration (SBC) checks that each posterior's credible
intervals have the coverage they claim, using fresh prior draws the network
never saw during training. For each of many simulated "true" parameter values
we compute where that value ranks among posterior draws conditioned on the
resulting simulated data; if the posterior is calibrated the rank is equally
likely to fall anywhere, so the rank histogram should come out flat. Skew
toward the edges means overconfidence; skew toward the middle, underconfidence.

```{r sbc}
sbc_res <- lapply(names(fits), function(nm)
  sbc(fits[[nm]], sims[[nm]]$sim, n_sbc = 150, n_posterior_samples = 300, seed = 2))
names(sbc_res) <- names(fits)
sbc_res$CBF
```

Because each prior's `mean` was a named vector, `sbc_res` carries those names
and `plot_sbc()` renders them as their plotmath symbol directly in the panel
title instead of a generic "parameter 3".

```{r sbc-beta0, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the intrinsic transmission rate in the CBF model."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res$CBF, param = 1L)
```

```{r sbc-r, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the CBF model's risk-averse transmission reduction r."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res$CBF, param = 4L)
```

```{r sbc-rho, fig.alt = "SBC rank histogram for the ascertainment rate rho in the CBF model."}
plot_sbc(sbc_res$CBF, param = 8L)
```

## Posterior predictive: daily incidence, three models

Now the comparison that matters. For each state we condition each trained
posterior on that state's own daily curve and population, push the draws back
through the simulator, and compare the predicted daily incidence to what was
reported.

We summarize the predictive by its **median**, not its mean. Daily counts here
span four orders of magnitude and the estimator is trained on \(\log(1+x)\);
the predictive distribution on each day is strongly right-skewed, so its mean
tracks the upper tail rather than the center. Plotting the mean of a skewed
count predictive makes a well-calibrated fit look like a systematic overshoot.

```{r post-pred, fig.width = 7.5, fig.height = 6.5, fig.alt = "Posterior predictive daily incidence for three models against reported cases in six representative states."}
highlight <- c("Washington", "New York", "California", "Louisiana", "Michigan", "West Virginia")
obs_for <- function(s) c(log(pop[[s]]), log1p(case_daily_s[s, ]))

pred_for <- function(nm, state, n_draws = 400) {
  post <- posterior(fits[[nm]], x_obs = obs_for(state))
  draws <- sample(post, n_draws)
  r <- sims[[nm]]$sim(draws, N_fixed = pop[[state]], full = TRUE)
  pred <- smooth7(r$reported)
  data.frame(model = nm, state = state, date = start_date + t_grid - 1,
             observed = case_daily_s[state, ],
             med = apply(pred, 2, stats::median),
             lo = apply(pred, 2, stats::quantile, 0.05),
             hi = apply(pred, 2, stats::quantile, 0.95))
}

pp <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(fits), function(nm)
  do.call(rbind, lapply(highlight, function(s) pred_for(nm, s)))))
pp$state <- factor(pp$state, levels = highlight)
pp$model <- factor(pp$model, levels = c("PW", "EFB", "CBF"))

library(ggplot2)
ggplot(pp, aes(date)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lo, ymax = hi, fill = model), alpha = 0.18) +
  geom_line(aes(y = med, colour = model), linewidth = 0.7) +
  geom_point(aes(y = observed), colour = "black", size = 0.35) +
  facet_wrap(~state, ncol = 2, scales = "free_y") +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = "new cases per day (7-day mean)",
       title = "Posterior predictive daily incidence, three models",
       subtitle = "points: reported cases; lines: predictive median; bands: 90% interval") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1),
        legend.position = "top")
```

A quantitative score across all 51 states, not just the six plotted: the
root-mean-square error of the predictive median against the observed curve, on
the \(\log(1+x)\) scale the estimator works in.

```{r model-scores}
score_state <- function(nm, state, n_draws = 300) {
  post <- posterior(fits[[nm]], x_obs = obs_for(state))
  r <- sims[[nm]]$sim(sample(post, n_draws), N_fixed = pop[[state]], full = TRUE)
  med <- apply(smooth7(r$reported), 2, stats::median)
  c(rmse = sqrt(mean((log1p(med) - log1p(case_daily_s[state, ]))^2)),
    attack = stats::median(1 - r$S_frac[, days]),
    R0 = stats::median(r$beta_eff[, 1]) / gamma_fixed)
}

scores <- do.call(rbind, lapply(names(fits), function(nm) {
  m <- t(sapply(state_names, function(s) score_state(nm, s)))
  data.frame(model = nm, rmse_med = median(m[, "rmse"]),
             rmse_iqr = paste(round(stats::quantile(m[, "rmse"], c(.25, .75)), 2), collapse = "-"),
             attack_med = median(m[, "attack"]),
             n_param = fits[[nm]]$dim_theta)
}))
scores
```

## R(t) for every state

The effective reproduction number is
\[
R_e(t) \;=\; \frac{\beta(t)}{\gamma} \cdot \frac{S(t)}{N},
\]
the average number of secondary cases per infection *given the susceptibles
still available*. The \(S(t)/N\) factor is not decoration: in New York the
model infers that a substantial share of the population had been infected by
mid-May, so \(R_e\) there ends the window well below what the transmission
rate alone would suggest. We compute both factors from the simulator's own
state trajectories rather than re-deriving \(\beta(t)\) alongside it, so the
reported quantity and the fitted model cannot drift apart. For the CBF model
\(\beta(t)\) is the transmission rate averaged over the susceptible pool,
which mixes the unprotected and risk-averse compartments.

```{r rt-all-states}
best <- as.character(scores$model[which.min(scores$rmse_med)])
best

rt_state <- function(state, nm = best, n_draws = 400) {
  post <- posterior(fits[[nm]], x_obs = obs_for(state))
  r <- sims[[nm]]$sim(sample(post, n_draws), N_fixed = pop[[state]], full = TRUE)
  rt <- r$beta_eff / gamma_fixed * r$S_frac
  data.frame(state = state, date = start_date + t_grid - 1,
             rt_med = apply(rt, 2, stats::median),
             rt_lo = apply(rt, 2, stats::quantile, 0.05),
             rt_hi = apply(rt, 2, stats::quantile, 0.95),
             attack = stats::median(1 - r$S_frac[, days]))
}

rt_all <- do.call(rbind, lapply(state_names, rt_state))
```

The inferred attack rates are the sanity check on the ascertainment prior:
what \(\rho\) and the case counts jointly imply about how much of each state
had actually been infected by 2020-05-19.

```{r attack-rates}
attack_by_state <- tapply(rt_all$attack, rt_all$state, `[`, 1)
round(quantile(attack_by_state, c(0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1)), 4)
round(sort(attack_by_state, decreasing = TRUE)[1:5], 3)
```

```{r rt-highlight, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5, fig.alt = "Effective reproduction number trajectories with 90 percent credible bands for six representative states."}
rt_h <- rt_all[rt_all$state %in% highlight, ]
rt_h$state <- factor(rt_h$state, levels = highlight)

ggplot(rt_h, aes(date, rt_med)) +
  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = rt_lo, ymax = rt_hi), fill = "steelblue", alpha = 0.25) +
  geom_line(colour = "steelblue", linewidth = 0.8) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = 2, colour = "grey40") +
  facet_wrap(~state, ncol = 3) +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = expression(R[e](t)),
       title = paste0("Posterior R_e(t) from the ", best, " model, 6 representative states"),
       subtitle = "shaded band: 90% credible interval; dashed line: R = 1") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.x = element_text(angle = 45, hjust = 1))
```

All 51 jurisdictions, condensed into one view.

```{r rt-heatmap, fig.width = 7, fig.height = 8, fig.alt = "Heatmap of posterior median effective reproduction number across all US states over the first 120 days."}
peak_order <- names(sort(tapply(rt_all$rt_med, rt_all$state, max), decreasing = TRUE))
rt_all$state <- factor(rt_all$state, levels = rev(peak_order))

ggplot(rt_all, aes(date, state, fill = rt_med)) +
  geom_raster() +
  scale_fill_gradient2(midpoint = 1, low = "steelblue", mid = "white",
                       high = "firebrick", name = expression(R[e](t))) +
  scale_x_date(date_labels = "%b %d") +
  labs(x = "date (2020)", y = NULL,
       title = paste0("Posterior median R_e(t) from the ", best, " model, all 50 states + DC"),
       subtitle = "sorted by peak; white = R = 1") +
  theme_minimal() +
  theme(axis.text.y = element_text(size = 6))
```

\(R_e(t)\) is flat and uninformative before a state's inferred introduction day
— no epidemic, nothing in the data to constrain transmission, so the posterior
there is close to the prior — then climbs through early-to-mid March as
community transmission spreads, and comes back down toward and below 1 by
mid-to-late April, with real heterogeneity in timing and magnitude that a
single national estimate would have hidden.

## What this comparison bought

The alternative to amortized NPE is fitting each model to each state
separately — 153 particle filters or MCMC chains, 153 opportunities for one to
fail to converge. Here, training happened three times (`r n_sim` simulations
each), and every state's posterior under every model cost a forward pass.

Three lessons generalize past this dataset.

*Do not leave the epidemic's phase to demographic noise.* A branching process
started from a couple of infections and run for four months has a final size
spread across orders of magnitude and a takeoff time that wanders by weeks. If
nothing in \(\theta\) can absorb that, the posterior predictive integrates over
it, and its mean sits far above the data while its 5th percentile sits at
zero. Inferring \((t_0, I_0)\) fixes it, and the recovered introduction days
are a free check on the change: the posterior puts New York's near March 1 and
West Virginia's in mid-March, matching when those states actually reported
their first cases.

*Do not let case counts alone set the epidemic's scale.* They identify
\(\rho \times \text{incidence}\). Without external information on \(\rho\) the
fit slides along that ridge toward vast, barely-ascertained epidemics, because
susceptible depletion is then available to flatten the peak. Seroprevalence
data break the tie, and they belong in the prior.

*A mechanism can be cheaper than a curve fit.* The piecewise model needs 10
parameters to bend \(\beta(t)\) into the right shape and, having no mechanism,
happily reports transmission rates for regimes that ended before the state's
epidemic began. The behavioral models get comparable fits from 6 and 8
parameters and put a physical interpretation on every one of them.

The models still carry real simplifications, worth stating plainly. \(\gamma\)
is fixed rather than inferred, so `sbc()` above is calibrated conditional on
that choice, not unconditionally. A single ascertainment rate \(\rho\) stands
in for what was, in practice, a patchwork of state and local testing capacity
that grew steeply over exactly this window; a time-varying \(\rho(t)\) is the
obvious next refinement, at the cost of a parameter confounded with
transmission. Each state gets one introduction, whereas states with large
international airports saw a trickle of imported cases for weeks before
community transmission established — California, whose first weeks carry a
handful of traveler-associated cases, is the state every model here fits
worst. The behavioral models assume the whole state reacts to a single
statewide case count, which is a strong assumption for California and a mild
one for Rhode Island. And Louisiana's Mardi Gras superspreading produced a
spike that a smooth behavioral response cannot reproduce. Loosening any of
these is a change to the simulator, not to the inference machinery around it —
the same amortized-NPE workflow applies unchanged.

For the underlying workflow in more depth, see `vignette("sir-epidemic")`,
`vignette("diagnostics")`, and `vignette("density-estimators")`.
